   "date": "2009-05-13T21:36:51Z",
   "subject": "Defining contour line levels for density2d",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "How do I go about setting the levels for the contour lines for<br>density2d?  If I use geom_tile, I can presumably do things like fill =<br>log(..density..) or fill = cut(..density.. , ...), but what if I want<br>to keep the contour line display but choose the values of the lines or<br>have them be at non-linear intervals?<p>Thanks!<p>           -s<br></p></p>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "nLhdlHn0dpkJ",
   "from": "JiHO",
   "author": "fc22cb727156ff4f",
   "date": "2009-05-13T22:21:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: Defining contour line levels for density2d",
   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "On 2009-May-13  , at 17:36 , Stavros Macrakis wrote:"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<p>&gt; How do I go about setting the levels for the contour lines for<br>&gt; density2d?  If I use geom_tile, I can presumably do things like fill =<br>&gt; log(..density..) or fill = cut(..density.. , ...), but what if I want<br>&gt; to keep the contour line display but choose the values of the lines or<br>&gt; have them be at non-linear intervals?</p>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<p><p>look at the &quot;breaks&quot; argument of stat_contour.<p>JiHO<br>---<br><a href=\"http://jo.irisson.free.fr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jo.irisson.free.fr/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792386032696000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1AvAOQ7uNvIco5pkX1Wz67\">http://jo.irisson.free.fr/</a><p></p></p></p></p>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "niHIodVu308J",
   "from": "David Kahle",
   "author": "e4aec126ff1dca22",
   "date": "2009-05-13T23:32:17Z",
   "subject": "Re: Defining contour line levels for density2d",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Hi Stavros - <div><br></div><div>I think ideally what you want is something akin to scale_y_log10() or scale_colour_manual() for the contour lines, right?  Unfortunately, there&#39;s not a scale_contour.  Looking at the source, it doesn&#39;t appear that there&#39;s any way to specify where the lines are, either by enumeration or a function to determine them like log10.  </div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve played around with the code for a little and learned from Hadley&#39;s source how to make a contour plot setting the levels manually, but my fix isn&#39;t pretty.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">library<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>MASS<span style=\"color:#001b93\">)</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147)\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">mu</span> &lt;- <span style=\"color:#000000\">c</span>(<span style=\"color:#224f1e\">0</span>,<span style=\"color:#224f1e\">0</span>)</div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147)\">(<span style=\"color:#000000\">si</span> &lt;- <span style=\"color:#000000\">matrix</span>(<span style=\"color:#000000\">c</span>(<span style=\"color:#224f1e\">3</span>,<span style=\"color:#224f1e\">2</span>,<span style=\"color:#224f1e\">2</span>,<span style=\"color:#224f1e\">2</span>), <span style=\"color:#224f1e\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#224f1e\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#000000\">byrow</span> = <span style=\"color:#000000\">T</span>))</div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">df<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> &lt;- </span>data.frame<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>mvrnorm<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span><span style=\"color:#224f1e\">1000</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>mu<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>mu<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>Sigma<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>si<span style=\"color:#001b93\">))</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">ggplot<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>aes<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>x<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>X1<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>y<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>X2<span style=\"color:#001b93\">), </span>data<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>df<span style=\"color:#001b93\">) +</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#001b93\">  </span>geom_point<span style=\"color:#001b93\">()</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">ggplot<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>aes<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>x<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>X1<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>y<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>X2<span style=\"color:#001b93\">), </span>data<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>df<span style=\"color:#001b93\">) +</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#001b93\">  </span>geom_density2d<span style=\"color:#001b93\">()</span></div><p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:12.0px Monaco;color:#001b93;min-height:16.0px\">  <br></p><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(79,79,79)\">## manually calculate the lines where you want them</div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">dens<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> &lt;- </span>kde2d<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>df<span style=\"color:#001b93\">$</span>X1<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>df<span style=\"color:#001b93\">$</span>X2<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>n<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span><span style=\"color:#224f1e\">100</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">)</span></div><p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:12.0px Monaco;color:#001b93;min-height:16.0px\">  <br></p><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">cl<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> &lt;- </span>contourLines<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147)\">  <span style=\"color:#000000\">x</span> = <span style=\"color:#000000\">dens</span>$<span style=\"color:#000000\">x</span>,</div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147)\">  <span style=\"color:#000000\">y</span> = <span style=\"color:#000000\">dens</span>$<span style=\"color:#000000\">y</span>,</div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147)\">  <span style=\"color:#000000\">z</span> = <span style=\"color:#000000\">dens</span>$<span style=\"color:#000000\">z</span>,</div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#001b93\">  </span>levels<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>fullseq<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>range<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>dens<span style=\"color:#001b93\">$</span>z<span style=\"color:#001b93\">), </span>.01<span style=\"color:#001b93\">)</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147)\">)  </div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">cl<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> &lt;- </span>mapply<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span><span style=\"color:#b98a23\">function</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>x<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>piece<span style=\"color:#001b93\">) {</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#001b93\">  </span>rbind<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>data.frame<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>x<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>piece<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>piece<span style=\"color:#001b93\">), </span>c<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span><span style=\"color:#b98a23\">NA</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span><span style=\"color:#b98a23\">NA</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span><span style=\"color:#b98a23\">NA</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">))</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#001b93\">  }, </span>cl<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>seq_along<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>cl<span style=\"color:#001b93\">), </span>SIMPLIFY<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span><span style=\"color:#b98a23\">FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">)</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">cl_df<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> &lt;- </span>do.call<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span><span style=\"color:#9c2211\">&#39;rbind&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>cl<span style=\"color:#001b93\">)</span></div><p style=\"margin:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;font:12.0px Monaco;color:#001b93;min-height:16.0px\">  <br></p><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(79,79,79)\">## make the plot with path  </div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\">ggplot<span style=\"color:#001b93\">() +</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#001b93\">  </span>geom_path<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>aes<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>x<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>x<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>y<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>y<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>group<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>piece<span style=\"color:#001b93\">, </span>colour<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>level<span style=\"color:#001b93\">),</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147)\">    <span style=\"color:#000000\">data</span> = <span style=\"color:#000000\">cl_df</span>) +</div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#001b93\">  </span>scale_colour_continuous<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>breaks<span style=\"color:#001b93\"> = </span>na.omit<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>unique<span style=\"color:#001b93\">(</span>cl_df<span style=\"color:#001b93\">$</span>level<span style=\"color:#001b93\">)))</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica\">As you can see, the levels are set by the levels = command within the contourLines command.  The sequence can, of course, be anything.</span></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Helvetica\"><br></font></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Helvetica\">Perhaps there&#39;s an easier way to do it, but this will get it done in a pinch.</font></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Helvetica\"><br></font></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Helvetica\">Cheers</font></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Helvetica\">david.</font></div><div style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;font:normal normal normal 12px/normal Monaco;color:rgb(0,27,147);min-height:16px\"><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div>On May 13, 2009, at 5:21 PM, JiHO wrote:</div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><br>On 2009-May-13  , at 17:36 , Stavros Macrakis wrote:<br><br><blockquote type=\"cite\">How do I go about setting the levels for the contour lines for<br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\">density2d?  If I use geom_tile, I can presumably do things like fill =<br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\">log(..density..) or fill = cut(..density.. , ...), but what if I want<br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\">to keep the contour line display but choose the values of the lines or<br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\">have them be at non-linear intervals?<br></blockquote><br>look at the &quot;breaks&quot; argument of stat_contour.<br><br>JiHO<br>---<br><a href=\"http://jo.irisson.free.fr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jo.irisson.free.fr/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792386032699000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1RS6N0wrs3caFjkgBDSXfz\">http://jo.irisson.free.fr/</a><br><br><br><br><br></div></blockquote></div><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "gylc7P0tJC8J",
   "from": "JiHO",
   "author": "fc22cb727156ff4f",
   "date": "2009-05-14T00:53:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: Defining contour line levels for density2d",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On 2009-May-13  , at 19:32 , David Kahle wrote:"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<p>&gt; ## manually calculate the lines where you want them<br>&gt;<br>&gt; dens &lt;- kde2d(df$X1, df$X2, n = 100)<br>&gt;</p>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<p><p>sorry for my first reply which overlooked the tiny problem that you  <br>can&#39;t have two stats for one geom. So David is correct, you have to  <br>compute the density outside of ggplot. However, once you do that,  <br>there is no need to do all the rest manually too. You can use  <br>geom_contour directly. See the second example in stat_density2d or :<p>library(&quot;ggplot2&quot;)<br>library(&quot;MASS&quot;)<p>d = data.frame(x=rnorm(1000),y=rnorm(1000))<p>dens = MASS::kde2d(d$x, d$y, n = 100)<br>densf = data.frame(expand.grid(x=dens$x, y=dens$y), z=as.vector(dens$z))<p>ggplot(densf, aes(x=x,y=y,z=z)) + geom_contour(breaks=seq(min(densf <br>$z), max(densf$z), length.out=10))<br># this puts 10 equally spaced breaks but you could put whatever you  <br>want in the breaks parameter<p>I hope this is more helpful.<p>JiHO<br>---<br><a href=\"http://jo.irisson.free.fr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jo.irisson.free.fr/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792386032700000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3snha3_kRvux9el4NlUeTA\">http://jo.irisson.free.fr/</a><p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "MuYv8WuXrgAJ",
   "from": "David Kahle",
